\details{
\code{exportPlot()} does not receive a finished plot but the plot \emph{call}. The
native pipe rewrites \code{f(x) |> exportPlot()} into \code{exportPlot(f(x))}, and
since R evaluates arguments lazily, \code{f(x)} has not been run when
\code{exportPlot()} starts. The call is captured with \code{\link[=substitute]{substitute()}} and
evaluated once per output file on a freshly opened device, and finally
once on the current device. Every file is therefore drawn at its own size
and resolution: margins, label widths and font sizes that a plot function
computes from the device (e.g. via \code{\link[=strwidth]{strwidth()}}) are right for each
format. Replaying a recorded plot (\code{\link[=recordPlot]{recordPlot()}}) would freeze the values
computed on the screen device instead.

This only works with the native pipe \verb{|>} or with a direct call
\code{exportPlot(plot(...), ...)}, which also accepts a braced block of several
drawing calls (see Examples). The magrittr pipe \verb{\%>\%} hands over a
placeholder, not the call.

\strong{Repeated evaluation.} Everything in \code{expr} runs once per output file
and once more on the screen if requested (\code{screen}): assignments,
messages, file operations and
expensive computations included. Prepare the data beforehand and pass
only the drawing calls.

\strong{Random numbers.} If a random number generator state exists, it is
restored before each evaluation, so every output starts from the same
state, and the state after the call is that of a single evaluation. A plot
that draws random numbers (jitter, sampling) then comes out the same in
every file, as long as it consumes the same random numbers on every
device; a plot that branches on device properties may not. In a fresh
session in which no random number has been drawn yet there is no state to
restore, and the outputs are not synchronised; call \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} first.

\strong{Graphics systems.} Base graphics are drawn by the call itself.
\code{ggplot} and \code{trellis} objects, which are only drawn when printed, are
printed on each device; a grob is drawn on a new page with
\code{\link[grid:grid.draw]{grid::grid.draw()}}. This applies only to a \emph{visible} result, as at the
console: \code{exportPlot(p, ...)} draws the ggplot \code{p}, while
\code{exportPlot(print(p), ...)} draws it once through \code{print()}, which
returns it invisibly. An object returned invisibly is never drawn
automatically.

\strong{Switch.} \code{create} defaults to the option \code{pharos.exportPlot}, which
is \code{FALSE} unless set. A script thus decides in one place whether its
figures are written, while single calls can still override it.

\strong{Devices.} Each file device is closed by its own number after drawing,
also if the plot call fails or opens or activates another device. The
device that was current before the call is made current again, provided
it is still open.
